{"answer":"The community watch is a rotating group of experienced fishers and elders who have agreed, on the Friday meetings, to take on enforcement.  Each day we assign three watchmen: two on the dock and one on the shore.  They carry a simple weigh‑stick and a whistle.  At the end of each trip, every fisher must bring the catch to the dock and hand it over to the nearest watchman.  The watchman weighs the catch and signs it off.  If a fisher has taken more than the 8 kg limit or if the daily 50 % cap has been reached, the watchman calls out the violation, the fisher must immediately return the excess weight (the fish are placed back in the lake or held for a community review), and the fishers are warned of the three‑day ban and penalty.  The watchmen have community‑approved authority to stop a fisher by stopping the line or by signaling to the group that no further fishing is allowed for the day.  Their power comes from the collective agreement made every Friday and is backed by the community’s tradition of mutual trust and the shared ledger that records every catch.

We also keep a small ledger on the dock where the watchman stamps each fisher’s name, weight, and time.  If the ledger shows that the cumulative weight has hit the 50 % cap, the watchmen shout a clear signal (a long whistle) and all nets are lowered.  The rule is simple: the watchmen enforce, the ledger records, and the community reviews on Friday.

Restating the proposal: "Each fisher may take no more than 8 kg per trip, and the community may not harvest more than 50 % of the lake's current stock in a day. All catch amounts will be recorded on a shared ledger after each trip. Each fisher’s quota of 8 kg is enforced by the community watch; once the daily 50% cap is reached, fishing stops for that day. If a fisher exceeds his quota or the daily cap, he must return the excess fish and will face a three‑day fishing ban and a community‑assigned penalty. The lake’s reserve is kept at 20% of its stock at all times, and every Friday the community will review the ledger, adjust quotas if needed, and communicate any changes via a notice board."","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take no more than 8 kg per trip, and the community may not harvest more than 50 % of the lake's current stock in a day. All catch amounts will be recorded on a shared ledger after each trip. Each fisher’s quota of 8 kg is enforced by the community watch; once the daily 50% cap is reached, fishing stops for that day. If a fisher exceeds his quota or the daily cap, he must return the excess fish and will face a three‑day fishing ban and a community‑assigned penalty. The lake’s reserve is kept at 20 % of its stock at all times, and every Friday the community will review the ledger, adjust quotas if needed, and communicate any changes via a notice board.","revised_operationalization":"The community watch consists of three rotating fishers/elders each day: two dock watchmen and one shore watchman.  They carry a weigh‑stick and a whistle.  At trip end, fishers bring their catch to the dock for weigh‑and‑sign.  The watchman verifies the 8 kg quota and the cumulative 50 % daily cap via the shared ledger.  If a violation occurs, the watchman signals (whistle) and requires the fisher to return excess weight immediately, imposes a three‑day ban and penalty, and lowers all nets for the rest of the day.  All enforcement actions are recorded in the ledger and communicated on the notice board at the Friday meeting.","reasoning":"I explained the watch’s composition, organization, and authority to make the answer clear and actionable while keeping the policy unchanged, as the question did not require any policy revision."}

